ere", "lp": -0.5659094452857971, "top": [[" where", -0.5659094452857971], [" the", -1.642680287361145]]}, {"t": " libraries", "lp": -0.0039451951161026955, "top": [[" libraries", -0.0039451951161026955], [" the", -6.195939064025879]]}, {"t": " go", "lp": -0.023455634713172913, "top": [[" go", -0.023455634713172913], [" are", -4.303217887878418]]}, {"t": " (`", "lp": -0.007142719812691212, "top": [[" (`", -0.007142719812691212], [" (", -5.205146312713623]]}, {"t": "lib", "lp": -0.028974220156669617, "top": [["lib", -0.028974220156669617], ["/", -3.583728551864624]]}, {"t": "`,", "lp": -0.17389215528964996, "top": [["`,", -0.17389215528964996], ["`", -2.3533642292022705]]}, {"t": " not", "lp": -0.2603605389595032, "top": [[" not", -0.2603605389595032], [" avoiding", -2.9337575435638428]]}, {"t": " `", "lp": -0.21206943690776825, "top": [[" `", -0.21206943690776825], [" the", -1.8482788801193237]]}], "text": "# PKGBUILD build() Function: Step-by-Step Explanation\n\nLet me break down this `build()` function line by line.\n\n## The Setup\n\n```bash\nsource /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh --force\n```\nThis loads the Intel oneAPI environment variables (compiler paths, library paths, etc.). The `--force` flag forces the environment to be set up even if it appears already configured. This is essential because we're using Intel's `icx`/`icpx` compilers, which aren't on the default PATH.\n\n```bash\nset -e\n```\nThis makes the script exit immediately if any command returns a non-zero status (an error). A safety net so a failed command doesn't cascade into broken output.\n\n## The CMake Configuration\n\nNow the main event \u2014 configuring the build with CMake:\n\n```bash\ncmake -S \"${srcdir}/llama.cpp\" -B \"${srcdir}/build\" -G Ninja\n```\n- `-S \"${srcdir}/llama.cpp\"` \u2014 **source directory** (where the source code lives)\n- `-B \"${srcdir}/build\"` \u2014 **build directory** (where build artifacts go)\n- `-G Ninja` \u2014 use the **Ninja** generator (fast, efficient build system) instead of the default Makefiles\n\n```bash\n-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release\n```\nSets the **build type** to Release. This enables optimizations (`-O3`-level flags) and disables debug symbols. (Note: `!debug` in options also strips debug info.)\n\n```bash\n-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr -DCMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR=lib\n```\n- **INSTALL_PREFIX** \u2014 where files get installed (`/usr`)\n- **INSTALL_LIBDIR** \u2014 where libraries go (`lib`, not `"}